  "response": "The quarrel over the Dark Ages was settled less by a single verdict than by a long reworking of the question, and its terms were set by Henri Pirenne. Pirenne attributed the rupture of Mediterranean trade networks to Arab expansion in the seventh century. His thesis held that the Germanic invasions of the fifth century ended Mediterranean economic unity, and these two datings of the break cannot both be true: a fifth-century terminus for Mediterranean unity rules out a terminus falling two hundred years later with the Arab conquests. Only after that thesis had been fully laid out did a second line of argument enter the field. Several historians argued that the apparent post-Roman decline reflected the loss of written sources rather than material impoverishment, and that argument led Peter Brown to popularise the concept of \"Late Antiquity\" as a period of cultural transformation. Academic historians now largely avoid the label \"Dark Ages\" in scholarly writing.\n\nThe documentary question is in part a matter of simple survival. At least one of two things holds, and perhaps both: the production of written documents declined in the post-Roman West, or large numbers of early medieval documents were destroyed in later centuries. Because the texts could not therefore be made to answer for themselves, the challenge mounted by those historians led directly to the adoption of methods that bypassed texts altogether, and radiocarbon dating allowed early medieval settlements to be dated without reference to written records.\n\nOnce material proxies entered the argument, the picture ceased to be uniform. Catalogued counts of Mediterranean shipwrecks fall sharply for the centuries after the fifth century; by contrast, Greenland ice cores show that lead pollution reached its ancient peak during the eighth century. Series of this kind measure different things, exchange in the one case and metallurgy in the other, and the discipline learned to weigh them separately rather than to assemble them into a single index of decline.\n\nThe Justinianic plague reached Constantinople in 541 CE; as a direct result of that arrival, woodland regenerated across parts of post-Roman Britain, as pollen sequences show. Dendrochronology supplies the chronological anchor, and here the evidence admits of exactly one of two readings: either European tree-ring sequences record sharply reduced growth in the 540s CE, that downturn being registered before the epidemic reached the imperial capital, or no tree-ring sequence records a growth downturn in the 540s CE at all. One of these two must hold of the rings, and they cannot both hold together. On the first reading the environmental and epidemiological records interlock closely, and it is that interlocking, rather than the silence of the chronicles, on which the modern verdict rests.",
